WEBVTT

NOTE
Journey: Advocates - Week 28 Leader Video
Unit 7, Lesson 4: Hell

1
00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:11.460
Several years ago, I was teaching one of my Bible classes and a really

2
00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:17.100
important and significant book in the lives of most evangelical Christians came out.

3
00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:22.880
And it created a firestorm of conversation, waves of controversy, and

4
00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:26.260
all kinds of things happening in the Christian world.

5
00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:28.860
The book was Love Wins by Rob Bell.

6
00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:33.580
And I had all kinds of parents asking me if we were going to read it or demanding

7
00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:37.160
that we don't read it. I had students asking me about it.

8
00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:40.220
So I finally said enough is enough.

9
00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:43.480
I need to figure out what the story is.

10
00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:48.480
So I read it and I used it as a discussion point for my students.

11
00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:54.160
Now the book had an incredible way of bringing up some really, really important

12
00:00:54.160 --> 00:01:00.160
topics to discuss. But what was interesting is that the students seemed

13
00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.760
to really gravitate toward what the book was suggesting.

14
00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:10.360
That we somehow have this endless sort of chance by chance or chance after chance

15
00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:15.780
cycle of getting to God. That if we don't, if we miss it the first time, we'll get a

16
00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.260
second, we'll get a second try at it.

17
00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:21.900
And that the idea of hell is really not all that accurate.

18
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:26.000
Now, it's an interesting conversation piece and something that we ought to talk

19
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.880
about. But the Bible is pretty clear.

20
00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:31.420
Now, we've talked about the reliability of this text.

21
00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:34.400
And Jesus is really clear that hell exists.

22
00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:40.240
But it's so easy for our students to want to discount that because we don't

23
00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:44.100
necessarily want to be held accountable to our actions.

24
00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:45.680
And we don't really want to face the consequences.

25
00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:46.920
I mean, who wants to face consequences?

26
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.960
So the question is often asked to me like, okay, well, what is hell like?

27
00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:53.620
And I don't know, it's probably not nice.

28
00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:56.020
I don't really know. I don't know if I want to know.

29
00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:02.160
It's not God. Hell is the tragic consequence of

30
00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:08.040
rejecting God. So to help your students understand what

31
00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:13.480
this tragic consequence would be like, the intro experience

32
00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:19.000
takes them through sort of a mini journey of imagining the best possible place, the

33
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:23.760
favorite place, why you love it, describe it, even put pictures in there,

34
00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:28.460
and then remove all of its goodness.

35
00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:30.360
Everything.

36
00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:34.620
Cross out any word that has good meaning.

37
00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:40.480
Remove all benefits from that place, all value, all joy, refreshing qualities, all

38
00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:43.860
of it. What would that place be like then?

39
00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:50.260
And that should help them get into that space of what hell would be like.

40
00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:56.460
I often describe it to students, think of it this way, common grace is

41
00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:59.100
the grace that God provides all of us, right?

42
00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:04.160
The fact that God is sustaining this world, every breath that I take, the sun that

43
00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:09.840
is shining, the air that is blowing, the trees growing, all the rain that falls, all

44
00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:15.600
of the things that happen on the righteous and the unrighteous are all

45
00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:21.260
common grace because God is here active and moving in history

46
00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:26.760
and participating in sort of sovereignly taking care of this world.

47
00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:32.340
Now remove all of that, and God is not doing any of that.

48
00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:38.000
Can you imagine what it might be like to not be able to take a breath?

49
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:43.220
earth to not have the sun shining to not have this earth sustained

50
00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:46.520
by him uninhabitable

51
00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:52.840
it's kind of the way I try to paint a picture for hell for students to give them

52
00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:58.620
that sense of like oh God is doing all of this for me whether I choose

53
00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:04.140
him or not right so then the question comes, well, wait a minute.

54
00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:07.640
How come God sends good people to hell?

55
00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:10.440
How come God sends anybody to hell?

56
00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:13.560
Well, the short answer is, is God doesn't send anybody.

57
00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:18.240
We send ourselves because we choose to not want him.

58
00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:24.480
You know, I've often said even that God honors our choices and allows us to choose

59
00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:26.620
not him and then gives us that very choice.

60
00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:31.420
So this week, small groups as always, but this week especially, is super

61
00:04:31.420 --> 00:04:35.840
important because all that we're talking about is sort of going to come to a bit of

62
00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:39.460
a culmination in the sense that how can a God that we've already said is good,

63
00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:44.760
that's allowing evil and all those kinds of things, like how can he then have the

64
00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:47.120
existence of hell be alongside that, right?

65
00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:51.600
If God is so good and so loving and he allows this kind of evil, this is the

66
00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:56.800
question that's been posed to me several times, is if God allows evil and

67
00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:02.760
allows the suffering to happen, and he's so good, then why would he have

68
00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:05.260
hell as a punishment for not believing?

69
00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:08.640
Almost seems like there's a contradiction, right?

70
00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:12.400
So these are some of the questions that your students may bring up or some version

71
00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:16.420
of. Again, open discussion. It's okay.

72
00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:22.080
If a student is not sure about hell, invite the discussion, invite the dialogue.

73
00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:27.240
Don't shoot them down and just point to Scripture and kind of thump on the

74
00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:28.720
Bible like, well, this is what the Bible says.

75
00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:32.980
Well, yeah, and we've talked about the reliability of the text and the authority of

76
00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:33.500
what's in there.

77
00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:39.900
But hell is a very, very real reality that can be difficult for some people to deal

78
00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:42.800
with. So be patient with your students as you bring them along if they're not quite

79
00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:45.840
there yet. Allow them the time to process it.

80
00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:49.000
And small groups are going to be one of those times where that's going to happen.

81
00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:55.560
And again, it's understanding that God wants us to choose him.

82
00:05:55.820 --> 00:06:00.980
God wants us to be with him. God invites us into a relationship, but he does not

83
00:06:00.980 --> 00:06:01.460
force us.

84
00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:07.480
And that is love in action. I want you to be with me.

85
00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:11.880
Imagine being married to somebody that you love that doesn't love you back.

86
00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:14.500
That doesn't seem to make any sense.

87
00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:18.840
When you made it through this week, and you've almost made it through the rest of

88
00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:19.220
the year,

89
00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:25.780
we continue to pray for you here, and we continue to pray for your students.

90
00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:31.840
And I hope that through this process of looking at hell

91
00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:37.320
and evil and suffering in a very, very real way has helped your students understand

92
00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:41.600
not only the point of suffering, but the character of God a little bit better.

93
00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:45.780
and that not only that this points to the existence of God in some sort of

94
00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:50.140
philosophical sense but that even in the midst of suffering God is right there with

95
00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:50.440
us

96
00:06:51.460 --> 00:06:56.860
feeling the pain along with us I hope your students have a better picture of who God

97
00:06:56.860 --> 00:07:01.800
is and who Christ is and what their salvation means and all of that you have four

98
00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:07.560
more lessons to go now would be a good time to take another inventory of your

99
00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:13.100
students and then where they are go back to some of your original notes about how

100
00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:18.680
your students started off this year and how they were around unit four where are

101
00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:24.600
they now and are you got some students that have made some decisions I hope and pray

102
00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:29.300
that you have do you have some students that still have significant questions if you

103
00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:32.900
need to take some time and revisit some of those questions either as a group or

104
00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:35.300
individually or in small groups, wherever you need to.

105
00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:41.500
Don't rush this process. You've got four lessons to go, all of which

106
00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:46.980
are gonna focus on wrapping things up and tying up some loose ends and helping to

107
00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:52.720
send your students onward and forward into the world with greater conviction, with

